When an AI agent does something that matters, several different systems will later have something to say about it. An identity system says who the agent was. A policy engine says the action was allowed. A sandbox says what the agent could reach. A trace says what the agent reported doing. A signed receipt says which bytes were issued. An evaluation says how the model behaved on tests. A regulation says what should have been true.

Each of those statements is useful. Each answers a different question, and each is made by a different party from a different vantage. This atlas lays them side by side: {{n_layers}} layers, {{n_entries}} named projects and standards, {{n_mechanisms}} mechanisms, and the public artifacts that work in the one cell no layer fills.

Every named project is either a standard from a recognized body or an organization-owned project measured at roughly 500 GitHub stars or more, and every figure carries its read date. The lists are representative, not exhaustive.

## The map in one table

{{layer_table}}

The layers map onto the three jobs that [*Three Jobs, Not One*](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21935891) separates. Layers 1 and 2 do the policy job: deciding what may be attempted. Layer 3 does the containment job: enforcing a boundary the code does not control. Layers 4 and 5 are where the evidence job is usually attempted, and the table shows why neither finishes it: a trace is written by the party it describes, and a signature fixes bytes without saying what they describe happened. Evaluation and governance are complementary roles. They scrutinize the other layers and cannot stand in for any of them.

## Who writes the record

The most useful single question to ask of any assurance mechanism is who writes the record it produces. The table uses the witness-scope terms of the [agent evidence vocabulary](https://github.com/probityai/agent-evidence-vocabulary): **self** is the observed party, or the system that runs it; **peer** is a party alongside it with its own stake, such as a gateway operator or an evaluator paid by the lab; **external** is a party outside the observed system's control. The assignment is this atlas's reading of a typical deployment, not a property of the software.

{{mechanism_table}}

Read down the "writer" column and one pattern repeats. Every mechanism that records the agent's actions in deployment is written by the agent's own side or by a peer with a stake in the outcome. Every mechanism written from outside records something other than those actions: an identity, a launch state, a log inclusion, a timestamp, a test result. The last row is the one this site is about.

{{layer_sections}}

## The open cell

Read the "cannot establish" column top to bottom and one question is never answered: **did this action happen as recorded, seen from a vantage the agent could not reach, and is anything missing?** Identity names the actor. Policy records the decision. Containment bounds the reach. Tracing reports what the actor said. Receipts fix the bytes. Evaluation measures a tested version. Governance says the answer should exist.

That cell is not closed by signing more things. A signature over a self-report is still a self-report. It is closed, where it can be closed at all, by records made from a vantage the observed party does not control, bound to what they observed, with an explicit statement of what they do not cover and a rule for what a missing record means. The [essay](essay.html) makes the argument; the [verifier demonstration](demo.html) shows what such records do and do not establish; the [experiments](experiments.html) measure where they fail.

## Where the public evidence artifacts sit

Five public repositories by the author work in this cell. Each entry says which layer it serves and, in its README's own terms, what it does not do.

